.TH WEBAUTHN 3
.SH NAME
webauthn \- the page's passkeys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #N /dev

.B /dev/webauthn
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On p9web,
.B #N
asks the browser that runs the system for a passkey's ceremony, for
.IR factotum (4)'s
.B webauthn
protocol.
A passkey belongs to the site the page came from, so it signs in only there.
.PP
Each open of
.B webauthn
takes one request, a line written to it:
.TP
.BI "get " "user rpid challenge credid" " ...
Sign in to
.I rpid
as
.I user
with one of the passkeys
.IR credid ,
or any of the site's if none is given.
.TP
.BI "create " "user rpid challenge userid
Make a passkey for
.I user
on
.IR rpid ,
under the user handle
.IR userid ,
which
.IR factotum (4)
chooses; the passkey is discoverable.
.PP
The binary fields are base64url, unpadded.
The page shows a bar that says what is asked, with a button that starts the browser's ceremony,
since the browser wants a click first.
The write returns when the ceremony ends, or fails with
.B canceled
if the user does not go on.
A read of the same open from offset 0, after a seek as for
.IR ndb (8)'s
.BR /net/cs ,
then gives the answer's line.
For
.BR get ,
it is
.IP
.I "credid authdata clientdata signature
.PP
and for
.BR create ,
.IP
.I "credid authdata clientdata spki alg
.PP
where
.I authdata
is the authenticator's data,
.I clientdata
the browser's JSON,
.I spki
the passkey's public key, and
.I alg
its COSE algorithm: -7 for ES256, -8 for Ed25519.
.PP
.B Webauthn
is the host owner's, mode 600.
Under Node, the tests' host is a software authenticator.
.SH SOURCE
.B k/src/kernel/authn.ts
.br
.B k/src/main/passkey.ts
.br
.B k/src/main/webauthnui.ts
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR factotum (4)
